# Original hypothetical cases, not patient reports or official exam questions.
# topic | stem | correct | three plausible alternatives | rationale | why alternatives fail
CASES={}
def cases(module,text):
 rows=[]
 for line in text.strip().splitlines():
  row=[x.strip() for x in line.split('|')];assert len(row)==8,(module,line);rows.append(row)
 CASES[module]=rows
cases(1, '''
Acquisition and proficiency | Ayesha heard Punjabi and Urdu regularly from infancy. At age nine she reads Urdu more confidently. Which description fits the information given? | Simultaneous acquisition with current skill-specific dominance | Sequential acquisition because reading skills are unequal | Receptive bilingualism because reading exceeds speaking | Subtractive bilingualism because the languages are unequal | Acquisition history and current proficiency answer different questions. Early overlapping exposure supports simultaneous acquisition; stronger Urdu reading identifies dominance for that skill. | Unequal literacy does not establish sequential onset, receptive-only ability or loss of Punjabi. No history of Punjabi decline is given.
Sequential acquisition | Hamza developed Punjabi at home before regular Urdu exposure began in preschool. Which term describes the acquisition sequence? | Sequential bilingual acquisition | Simultaneous bilingual acquisition | Balanced bilingual proficiency | Receptive bilingual proficiency | Sequential describes a later language beginning after earlier development has started; successive and consecutive are common alternative labels. | Simultaneous does not match the stated history. Balanced and receptive describe abilities, which cannot be determined from onset alone.
Additive versus balanced | A Lahore school adds English instruction while supporting Urdu learning. A pupil remains much stronger in Urdu. Which conclusion is justified? | The arrangement can be additive despite unequal proficiency | Additive development is excluded until both scores are equal | Unequal proficiency establishes subtractive development | Stronger Urdu shows that English learning has not begun | Additive concerns maintaining an earlier language while adding another. Balance concerns comparable proficiency under specified measures. | Equality is not required for an additive orientation. Unequal skill alone does not show loss or failure to learn.
Subtractive conditions | After repeated criticism of Punjabi, a child stops using it and gradually loses access to familiar expressions while English use expands. Which process best fits? | Subtractive language development under social pressure | Balanced bilingualism across all communication domains | Simultaneous acquisition of two first languages | Productive use increasing equally in both languages | The defining evidence is reduced Punjabi use and accessibility while a socially rewarded language expands. | Balance and equal growth contradict the account. Simultaneous acquisition concerns onset, which the case does not describe.
Receptive ability | Bilal understands his grandmother's Punjabi requests and responds appropriately in Urdu. What is the most precise description of the observed Punjabi skill? | Receptive ability exceeds observed production | Comprehension is absent because he answers in Urdu | Productive Punjabi proficiency is already established | Punjabi has necessarily been lost through attrition | Correct responses indicate understanding; they do not demonstrate equivalent production in Punjabi. | Language choice does not establish absent comprehension, fluent Punjabi output or prior loss. Investigate opportunity and preference separately.
Dominance across domains | Sana knows medical terminology in English but family kinship terms more readily in Urdu. Which interpretation is strongest? | Language strengths are distributed across domains | English must dominate every aspect of her repertoire | Urdu must be her only conceptually developed language | Domain differences indicate incomplete bilingual competence | Different experiences support different resources. The complementarity principle explains domain-related strengths without assuming failure. | One domain cannot establish global dominance or invalidate the other language. Neither profile is evidence of a disorder.
First language and dominance | An adult first learned Saraiki but now conducts most professional and social activity in Urdu. What extra evidence is needed to identify current dominance? | Comparative abilities and use in relevant domains | The language first acquired, without further assessment | The language named first on an identity document | The language with the larger national population | First acquisition is historical; dominance is a current, task-dependent profile. | Order, administrative labels and population size do not directly measure the individual's present skills.
Multilingual conventions | One textbook includes bilingual people under multilingualism; a slide reserves multilingualism for three or more languages. How should an exam explanation handle this? | State the convention used and distinguish the two usages | Treat the narrower usage as the only linguistic definition | Treat the broader usage as evidence that trilingualism is invalid | Infer that bilingualism and monolingualism are interchangeable | Both conventions occur. An unambiguous answer identifies the definition used in that question. | Neither usage invalidates the other labels. Monolingualism remains distinct from knowledge or use of two languages.
Individual and societal levels | A Karachi neighborhood has several language communities, although one resident uses only Urdu. Which description is consistent? | Societal multilingualism with an individually monolingual resident | Individual multilingualism must hold for every resident | The neighborhood must be monolingual because one resident is | Multilingual society requires equal proficiency in all local languages | Community diversity and an individual's repertoire are separate units of description. | A single person's skills do not determine the entire community profile, and societal diversity does not imply universal individual proficiency.
Modality | A hearing adult uses spoken Urdu and Pakistan Sign Language. Which classification concerns this difference in modality? | Bimodal bilingualism | Unimodal bilingualism | Receptive-only bilingualism | Balanced bilingualism | A spoken and a natural signed language use different broad modalities. | Unimodal would involve the same broad modality. Receptive and balanced classifications require different evidence about ability.
Biliteracy | A student converses fluently in Punjabi and Urdu but has learned to read only Urdu. Which statement is supported? | Oral bilingualism is established; biliteracy is not established | Biliteracy follows from fluent conversation in both languages | Punjabi cannot be a language without reading ability | Urdu literacy establishes balanced proficiency in both languages | Oral language and literacy are related but distinct; reading and writing require relevant learning opportunities. | Literacy cannot be inferred from conversation, and a person's literacy does not determine a language's legitimacy or overall balance.
Code-switching | In an Urdu conversation, a lecturer quotes a complete English sentence and then returns to Urdu. Under the broad alternation definition, which analysis fits best? | Code-switching across stretches of discourse | Monolingual speech with an altered accent | Attrition of the entire earlier language system | Biliteracy established by the spoken quotation | The observed event is alternation between languages within discourse. | Accent is pronunciation variation, attrition concerns reduced access, and a spoken quotation does not demonstrate reading and writing in two languages.
Code-mixing | Using the convention that code-mixing refers to insertion within an utterance, which example best illustrates it? | An English technical noun inserted into an Urdu sentence | A shift from Urdu to English between two complete interview sections | Punjabi pronunciation differing across two villages | An English paragraph translated into a separate Urdu paragraph | The question states its convention explicitly, making insertion within an utterance the intended distinction. | The interview illustrates a discourse-level switch; regional pronunciation concerns accent; translation is a different operation. Other authors may classify insertion as code-switching too.
Borrowing | A loanword is conventional in local Urdu and is used by speakers who do not know its source language. What does this establish? | Borrowing can become part of community usage | Each speaker must be productively bilingual | The word remains an individual switch in every occurrence | Urdu has ceased to possess its own linguistic system | Community incorporation distinguishes an established borrowing from the inference that each current user alternates between two languages. | Source-word history does not demonstrate the speaker's bilingual ability, and contact does not erase the receiving language's system.
Translanguaging | Students discuss a concept using Urdu and Punjabi before writing an English explanation. The teacher explicitly teaches the required English genre. Which analysis is most appropriate? | Repertoire use supports learning alongside a specified output goal | A flexible discussion prevents any English learning objective | Mixed discussion proves that students lack the underlying concept | English writing can be assumed correct without instruction | Flexible multilingual meaning-making can coexist with explicit expectations for a particular audience and genre. | Neither confusion nor absence of an English goal follows. The final English forms still need teaching and assessment.
Vocabulary distribution | A child names farming tools in Punjabi and classroom materials in English. What should a clinician investigate before interpreting one low naming score? | Knowledge and learning opportunities across relevant languages | Whether English is institutionally more prestigious | Which language has the older written tradition | Whether every family member uses one language exclusively | Distribution of experience can explain language-specific vocabulary; a single-language score is not the total conceptual repertoire. | Prestige, literary age and a rigid family arrangement do not resolve what the child knows across contexts.
Shift versus attrition | Across three generations, a family increasingly uses only the school language. Which label best describes the changing family pattern? | Intergenerational language shift | A momentary lexical retrieval failure | Simultaneous acquisition in every generation | A single speaker's pronunciation accommodation | Shift concerns changing patterns of use across time and generations. Attrition could occur in individuals, but is not identical to the family-level pattern. | The alternatives describe a brief event, an unsupported onset history or an interactional adjustment rather than the observed generational trend.
Attrition | After years of little Punjabi use, an adult has slower retrieval of familiar Punjabi words but retains useful understanding. Which inference is defensible? | Selective reduced accessibility is compatible with attrition | The entire language representation has necessarily disappeared | The person was never bilingual at any earlier time | Current understanding proves that no attrition could occur | Attrition can affect accessibility or specific skills without complete erasure. | Residual understanding is compatible with selective loss, while current difficulty does not invalidate past bilingualism.
Heritage language | A British Pakistani teenager strongly identifies with family Punjabi but reads English better. Which conclusion follows? | Heritage affiliation does not require dominant literacy | Punjabi cannot be a heritage language if reading is weaker | English literacy establishes loss of Punjabi comprehension | Family affiliation requires simultaneous acquisition from birth | Heritage connection, reading ability and acquisition sequence are different dimensions. | Weaker literacy does not cancel affiliation, establish comprehension loss or prove a particular onset history.
Language-maintenance planning | Which plan most directly supports a child's heritage-language maintenance? | Regular meaningful interaction with willing fluent relatives | Displaying the language name without opportunities to use it | Requiring identical weekly scores in every language skill | Delaying all interaction until formal spelling is mastered | Sustained relationships and useful opportunities to communicate support continued use. | Symbolic display alone is not practice; equal scores are not the definition of maintenance; oral interaction need not wait for literacy.
Metalinguistic awareness | A child explains that kitab and book are different forms referring to the same type of object. What is being demonstrated? | Reflection on the relationship between form and meaning | Perfect literacy in both writing systems | A general advantage in nonverbal intelligence | Loss of the distinction between the two languages | The child is treating language forms as objects of reflection and comparing their meanings. | The observation does not test reading, general intelligence or confusion; such claims exceed the evidence.
Evidence and confounding | A study compares highly educated bilingual adults with monolingual adults who had less schooling, then attributes a reasoning difference to bilingualism. What is the central problem? | Education is a plausible confounding difference | Every group difference must be a language disorder | Bilingualism cannot be studied through any comparison | Reasoning tasks directly measure balanced proficiency | The groups differ on another plausible influence on performance. A stronger design would address relevant background differences. | Confounding limits attribution; it does not diagnose disorder, forbid research or turn reasoning into a direct language-proficiency measure.
Historical deficit labels | An assessor calls a child semilingual after testing unfamiliar vocabulary only in English. What is the strongest critique? | The label exceeds evidence from one language and ignores opportunity | The label is valid whenever the home language differs from school | Equal low scores would independently explain every cause | Mixing languages is a sufficient second diagnostic test | A detailed profile is needed; an unsupported deficit label obscures what the child has had opportunities to learn. | Language background or mixing is not a diagnosis, and even multiple low scores require valid comparison and contextual interpretation.
Language-history interview | Which question most directly helps explain domain-specific vocabulary in a multilingual child? | Who uses each language with the child, for which activities, and since when? | Which named language is considered most prestigious nationally? | Which language does the assessor personally find easiest to score? | Which single family language should be treated as the child's identity? | Partners, timing and activities connect exposure with the vocabulary and skills likely to develop. | Prestige, assessor convenience and an imposed identity do not describe the child's actual learning history.
Integrated profile | Zain speaks Urdu and Punjabi, writes only Urdu, and recently began learning English. Which description stays within the evidence? | Bilingual oral resources, Urdu literacy and emerging additional-language learning | Equal trilingual proficiency across oral and written tasks | Receptive Punjabi with no productive evidence | Simultaneous acquisition of all three languages | The profile separates observed oral, literacy and emerging additional-language abilities. | Equal proficiency and common onset are not given; Punjabi speaking is productive evidence, so receptive-only is inaccurate.
''')
cases(2, '''
Difference and disorder | A child recently entering an English-medium school communicates effectively in Punjabi. English errors follow patterns expected from Punjabi experience. What is the best next step? | Build a broader profile before attributing the errors to impairment | Diagnose disorder from the English errors alone | Conclude that good Punjabi excludes every possible disorder | Stop Punjabi use before gathering further information | The history makes language difference plausible, but an appropriate assessment still considers learning opportunities, concerns and functioning across contexts. | One English pattern is insufficient for diagnosis; good functioning in one context is not an absolute exclusion; removing Punjabi does not supply the missing evidence.
Cross-language manifestations | Two languages mark grammar differently. Which finding is most consistent with investigating a possible disorder? | Persistent difficulties interpreted against expectations appropriate to each language | Identical surface errors required in every language | An English-specific suffix error required in Punjabi too | Failure on an untranslated English test treated as sufficient | Difficulties may manifest differently because language structures and experience differ. The assessment must address the repertoire and relevant expectations. | Identical forms are not expected across distinct systems, and one poorly matched test does not establish the cause of difficulty.
Exposure quality | Two children hear the same estimated hours of Urdu. One has responsive conversations; the other mainly hears background media. What should be concluded? | Exposure duration alone does not capture interaction quality | Both have equivalent learning conditions by definition | Passive listening necessarily provides more communicative practice | The conversational child must have balanced bilingual proficiency | Accessible, contingent exchanges differ from background exposure; duration is one part of the learning profile. | Equal hours do not establish equal experience or outcomes, and neither condition proves a complete proficiency classification.
Home-language advice | A Punjabi-speaking mother communicates most naturally in Punjabi but is told to use only her limited English with her child. Which response is most appropriate? | Support meaningful family communication while planning needed English opportunities | Enforce English-only use because it is the school language | Guarantee identical English outcomes regardless of interaction | Avoid discussing language preferences with the family | Family language resources matter. A collaborative plan can support the home language and additional-language access. | School status does not justify a blanket ban; outcomes cannot be guaranteed; excluding family preferences weakens planning.
Family language strategies | Which outcome would justify adapting a family's one-person-one-language arrangement? | The family reports that the arrangement limits meaningful interaction | One child uses different words across languages | A grandparent uses the heritage language comfortably | Two caregivers differ in occupation | Strategies should serve communication needs and fit real relationships rather than function as rigid rules. | Distributed words are expected; a comfortable grandparent relationship can be useful; occupation alone does not establish a problem with the arrangement.
Concept and word learning | An SLP teaches the concept of clothing categories but never teaches the child the words needed to request a coat. Which gap remains? | Functional language-specific labels and expressions | A need to avoid categories in all bilingual work | Proof that conceptual teaching is inherently ineffective | A requirement to wait for equal bilingual vocabulary totals | Understanding a category and accessing useful labels are related but distinct targets. Practice can connect both to real requests. | The problem does not invalidate categories or conceptual learning, and equal totals are not a prerequisite for useful communication.
Bilingual approach | Which target most naturally illustrates an intervention focus on a shared skill across languages? | Organizing a narrative around a problem, action and outcome | Teaching an English-only grammatical suffix as if it existed in Urdu | Translating a test and preserving its norms without evidence | Practicing only the therapist's preferred language regardless of needs | Narrative organization can be a shared target, while actual forms and culturally appropriate storytelling still require attention. | The suffix is language-specific, test translation is not a shared-skill target, and convenience is not a justified bilingual plan.
Cross-linguistic approach | A child needs different sound targets because the languages have different sound inventories. Which plan is most defensible? | Select language-appropriate targets and monitor relevant contrasts | Apply one language's sound inventory as the norm for both | Treat every cross-language contrast as disordered | Assume an English target must transfer equally to all contexts | A cross-linguistic approach recognizes specific linguistic systems and the child's actual needs. | Different inventories are not disorders, one norm does not automatically fit both, and transfer must be measured.
Contrastive reasoning | A grammatical distinction used in English is expressed differently in the child's home language. What does this information provide? | A hypothesis about an expected cross-linguistic influence to investigate | Proof of impairment whenever the English marking differs | Evidence that the home language lacks meaningful grammar | A reason to stop collecting individual language samples | Comparison helps interpret patterns, but individual evidence remains necessary. | Structural differences do not establish deficiency or remove the need for assessment.
Cross-language generalization | After narrative intervention in Urdu, which observation directly supports transfer to English? | Improved performance on an appropriate untrained English narrative task | Improved repetition of the exact trained Urdu story | More therapy attendance without English measurement | Caregiver preference for English-medium education | The evidence must include change in the untrained language on a relevant measure. | Practiced Urdu performance, attendance and preference do not directly measure English transfer.
Within-language generalization | A child practices one Urdu request and later uses its structure with new Urdu objects. Which change is directly demonstrated? | Generalization to new items within Urdu | Transfer to an unmeasured second language | Acquisition of a new language from infancy | Normalization of all standardized test scores | New items within the same language demonstrate within-language generalization. | Other-language change, acquisition history and global scores require different evidence.
Baseline and monitoring | Which design best distinguishes an initial low level from improvement during therapy? | Comparable target probes before and during the intervention | A single successful rehearsed response after therapy | An attendance total with no performance measure | A post-treatment score interpreted without starting performance | Baseline and repeated comparable observations show the trajectory of performance. | One response can reflect practice; attendance is not performance; a post-score alone does not show individual change from baseline.
Dynamic assessment | Which procedure most clearly examines learning potential? | Assess a novel task, mediate learning, then assess response and transfer | Repeat a static score and average the two results without teaching | Translate every item and apply the original norms unchanged | Rank children only by unfamiliar English naming totals | Test-teach-retest makes the learning response and support required observable. | Repetition alone is not mediation, translation does not validate norms, and static unfamiliar-language ranking does not directly assess modifiability.
Modifiability | Two children start with similar scores. One applies a taught strategy with little support; the other needs extensive support. What is most directly compared? | Modifiability during supported learning | An identical level of bilingual balance | The number of languages known by each family | A definitive diagnosis from the initial score alone | The relevant observation concerns responsiveness and the effort needed during mediation. | It does not directly measure balance or family repertoire, and no single score provides a complete diagnosis.
Vocabulary calculation | A child knows 28 Urdu labels and 22 English labels. Ten translation-equivalent pairs overlap and there are no other duplicated concepts. What are total and conceptual vocabulary respectively? | 50 and 40 | 40 and 50 | 28 and 22 | 60 and 50 | Total word forms = 28 + 22 = 50. Unique concepts = 50 − 10 = 40 under the stated assumptions. | Reversing the values confuses conventions; reporting separate-language totals does not answer the question; adding overlap double-counts concepts.
Normative validity | An English test is translated informally for a Punjabi-speaking child. Which report is most defensible? | Describe observed performance and document why original standard scores may be invalid | Present original percentiles because item meanings seem similar | Replace unfamiliar words while retaining all norms automatically | Interpret translation accuracy as proof of population equivalence | Translation and altered administration can change task properties. Standardized interpretations need relevant validity evidence. | Similar meaning, replacement or a fluent translator does not establish equivalent difficulty, measurement or norms.
Language sampling | A child performs adequately on naming but has concerns in conversation. What additional evidence is most relevant? | Connected-language samples with appropriate partners and contexts | More repetitions of the same isolated labels only | The institutionally preferred accent of the examiner | The number of languages named on a school form alone | Conversation and narrative can reveal organization and use not measured by naming. | Repeating labels does not sample the reported difficulty; examiner prestige and language counts do not substitute for functional evidence.
Interpreter role | An Urdu-speaking clinician assesses a Hindko-speaking child with a suitable interpreter. Who retains responsibility for the clinical conclusion? | The clinician, informed by collaborative linguistic evidence | The interpreter alone because the child shares that language | The family alone because professional comparison is impossible | Whoever can calculate the test score fastest | Interpreter expertise supports access and interpretation, while the clinician retains clinical responsibility and acknowledges limits. | Shared language does not transfer clinical responsibility; neither family responsibility nor calculation speed resolves professional judgment.
Translation versus interpretation | A clinic needs a written Urdu appointment leaflet and a live Punjabi consultation. Which arrangement matches the tasks? | Written translation for the leaflet and interpretation for the consultation | Interpretation for the written leaflet and no support for the consultation | Clinical diagnosis by the translator and scoring by the family | One written translation treated as sufficient for all spoken discussion | Translation concerns written content; interpretation supports live spoken or signed communication. | Task mismatch or unsupported role substitution does not provide adequate communication access.
Cultural broker | A collaborator explains why a family interprets one appointment routine differently. How should that information be used? | As context to explore with the family rather than a fixed rule for all members | As proof that every family from that region has the same preference | As a replacement for asking the client about consent | As a clinical diagnosis independent of assessment | Cultural knowledge can inform questions, but the particular person's experience remains central. | Group generalizations do not establish individual preferences, consent or a diagnosis.
Hearing consideration | A bilingual child has communication concerns at home and school. Which approach avoids assuming one explanation? | Consider hearing access and development alongside language opportunities | Attribute all concerns to using two languages | Exclude hearing evaluation because the child sometimes responds | Infer normal language from the number of languages heard | Relevant hearing and developmental information helps distinguish possible contributors. | Bilingualism is not a sufficient cause; occasional responses do not establish full hearing access; exposure counts are not a language assessment.
Functional outcome | Which outcome most directly measures a family-prioritized communication goal? | The child independently asks grandparents for help during a familiar routine | The child completes more worksheets in the clinic | The child adopts the therapist's accent during one imitation | The family reports that the appointment room is attractive | The target concerns useful communication with the relevant partners in the valued activity. | Worksheets and imitation may provide narrower evidence, while room preference does not measure the communication goal.
Caregiver coaching | A parent misses written homework because the sheet is inaccessible in language and literacy level. What should change first? | Agree an understandable, feasible routine and check shared understanding | Increase worksheet length to establish commitment | Infer that home-language use prevents cooperation | Stop discussing goals because attendance is the only relevant outcome | Coaching should fit the family's communication resources and daily circumstances. | More inaccessible work, blame or removal of collaboration does not address the stated barrier.
Different meanings of accommodation | A lecturer discusses changing a mental category after encountering contradictory evidence. Which meaning of accommodation is intended? | Piagetian modification of a cognitive scheme | Temporary alteration of test presentation for access | Speech adjustment toward a conversational partner | Permanent replacement of a heritage language | The question specifies a change in a mental category, locating the term in schema theory. | Test accommodations, communication accommodation and sociocultural assimilation are different uses requiring separate context.
Treatment decision | A family needs Urdu for school and Punjabi for caregiving, while the SLP speaks Urdu only. Which plan best addresses the information given? | Collaborate on priorities and arrange suitable support for Punjabi needs | Treat Urdu alone as sufficient because it is the clinician's language | Presume all Urdu gains will appear equally in Punjabi without probes | Exclude family communication because school performance is easier to score | Planning should reflect both meaningful settings and realistic professional support, with monitoring of relevant languages. | Convenience, assumed transfer and exclusion of a stated need do not adequately justify the language plan.
''')
cases(3, '''
BICS and CALP | Mariam chats confidently in English at break but struggles to explain a research finding in an English paragraph. What is the most appropriate interpretation? | Conversational ease does not establish academic-language proficiency for that task | Fluent conversation proves that writing difficulty is motivational | The two tasks measure identical demands because both use English | Her Urdu knowledge must be preventing academic English development | Academic explanation can require explicit organization, technical vocabulary and reasoning relationships beyond familiar conversation. | Motivation, identical demand and harmful Urdu influence are not established by this contrast.
Context and reasoning | A teacher shows a working model of lungs and asks learners to compare two explanations of airflow. Which combination is most evident? | Strong contextual support with substantial cognitive demand | Little contextual support with minimal cognitive demand | Strong contextual support with necessarily minimal reasoning | Little contextual support because the content is scientific | The visible model provides cues; comparing explanations requires reasoning. Contextual support and cognitive demand are different dimensions. | Scientific content does not remove visible support, and support does not imply an intellectually trivial task.
Context-reduced task | Which activity provides the fewest immediate situational cues, assuming equally unfamiliar content? | Reading a written explanation without pictures or interaction | Explaining a process while manipulating its visible materials | Following directions during a live demonstration | Asking clarification questions about an object both partners can see | The written account lacks the immediate objects, demonstration and feedback available in the other conditions. | The alternatives contain shared context or interaction. Prior knowledge could still support any task, so the assumption about familiarity matters.
Scaffolding | Which sequence best illustrates support moving toward independence? | Model an explanation, guide attempts, then reduce prompts as control grows | Give the completed explanation on every attempt and infer independence | Remove all support before identifying what the learner understands | Retain identical prompts regardless of the learner's progress | Scaffolding responds to need and gradually transfers responsibility to the learner. | Permanent substitution, premature removal and unresponsive prompting do not demonstrate that progression.
Silent period | A child says little in a new school language but communicates well at home. Which action is most defensible? | Examine comprehension, participation and exposure while monitoring concerns | Diagnose disorder because school production is limited | Wait a fixed number of years regardless of other concerns | Infer that all conceptual reasoning has paused | Reduced new-language output can have several explanations. Contextual review and appropriate follow-up avoid both premature diagnosis and indefinite waiting. | Limited output is not sufficient diagnosis, a universal waiting rule or evidence of stopped reasoning.
Stage approximation | The slide lists 1,000 words for early production. A learner's score differs. What can be concluded from that fact alone? | A teaching approximation has not established an individual diagnostic cutoff | A disorder is confirmed below that exact number | The learner must immediately move to advanced fluency above that number | The number identifies the learner's full conceptual knowledge across languages | Fixed stage counts depend on definitions and methods and do not independently diagnose or classify an individual. | A single count does not establish disorder, a stage transition or the full multilingual repertoire.
Stage order | Under the lecture's broad instructional sequence, which stage follows early production? | Speech emergence | Advanced fluency | Pre-production | Intermediate fluency | The listed order is pre-production, early production, speech emergence, intermediate fluency and advanced fluency. It is a guide, not a rigid individual timetable. | The alternatives occupy earlier or later positions in that specific teaching model.
Intermediate fluency | A learner uses complex sentences but asks for clarification of unfamiliar academic expressions. Which interpretation fits the lecture's intermediate-fluency description? | Increasing complexity with continuing support needs | Perfect command of every academic genre | Absence of comprehension because clarification is requested | A necessary return to pre-production in all settings | More complex use can coexist with gaps and clarification needs. | A request for help does not establish absent comprehension or erase abilities in other settings, and advanced knowledge is not unlimited.
Advanced proficiency | A fluent graduate encounters unfamiliar legal terminology. What does this show? | Proficiency remains sensitive to domain knowledge and experience | The graduate could never have achieved advanced language use | A single unfamiliar term establishes generalized impairment | Academic vocabulary is unrelated to genre and field | Even advanced users learn specialized terms and discourse conventions in new fields. | Local unfamiliarity does not invalidate prior proficiency or diagnose impairment; the field and genre are relevant.
CUP transfer | A child explains cause and effect in Urdu but lacks the relevant English connectives. Which teaching plan best uses an existing resource? | Connect the known relationship to English expressions through meaningful tasks | Assume the English expressions emerge automatically without input | Reteach all reasoning as if no conceptual knowledge exists | Exclude Urdu explanation from evidence of understanding | The concept can support learning, while language-specific forms still require accessible input and practice. | Automatic transfer and denial of prior knowledge are both unwarranted; excluding relevant evidence conceals a useful resource.
Iceberg model | Which inference should NOT be drawn from the two-iceberg illustration? | It locates two literal fused language structures in a specific brain region | Some underlying knowledge can support more than one language | Surface forms can differ despite shared conceptual resources | Cross-language support depends on relevant learning conditions | The iceberg is a conceptual metaphor rather than a neuroanatomical map. | The remaining statements express qualified conceptual interpretations rather than a literal localization claim.
Demand matrix | A teacher adds diagrams to a difficult explanation without reducing the reasoning required. Which change is most directly intended? | Increase contextual support while retaining cognitive challenge | Convert all abstract reasoning into a memorized greeting | Reduce the learner's existing first-language knowledge | Prove that the task no longer needs academic language | Diagrams can make meaning more accessible while comparison and explanation remain demanding. | Support does not inherently trivialize reasoning, remove prior knowledge or eliminate academic-language demands.
Comprehensible input | Which change most directly improves access to meaning during new-language instruction? | Link manageable language with a familiar demonstration and check understanding | Increase exposure length while leaving meaning inaccessible | Replace interaction with faster presentation of unfamiliar sentences | Judge comprehension solely from whether the learner stays quiet | Meaningful connection and checks matter in addition to exposure quantity. | Longer or faster inaccessible input and silence alone do not establish understanding.
Negotiation of meaning | During a task, Ali asks which box his partner means; the partner points and rephrases. What process is most directly illustrated? | Negotiation of meaning | Intergenerational language shift | Historical standardization | A fixed level of bilingual balance | The partners resolve a communication problem through clarification and reformulation. | The alternatives concern community change, institutional norms or a proficiency comparison, none of which is established here.
Wait time | After a question, a learner pauses and then gives a thoughtful answer. Which interpretation is most defensible? | Formulation and processing time need not imply lack of understanding | A pause is a direct measure of low intelligence | Every pause demonstrates complete comprehension | Response speed alone determines acquisition stage | Time can reflect processing, formulation, confidence and task demands; assess the response and context. | Neither deficit nor full understanding follows from latency alone, and speed is not a complete stage measure.
Language anxiety | A learner speaks freely with one trusted partner but rarely in an assessed group. What should be investigated? | How evaluation and social conditions affect participation | Whether silence proves equally limited knowledge in all settings | Whether a single fixed stage can replace contextual information | Whether the home language should be removed as the presumed cause | Variation by setting suggests considering participation conditions, including anxiety, rather than equating silence with absent knowledge. | A global deficit, stage label or language ban is not established by this contrast.
Academic vocabulary | Which target most directly supports writing a cause-and-effect explanation? | Meaningful use of because, therefore and evidence in an explanation | Repeating greetings already used fluently with friends | Memorizing the alphabet as a complete substitute for organization | Counting languages without examining writing performance | The target connects relational vocabulary to the required academic purpose. | Familiar greetings, isolated letters and language counts do not directly teach the stated explanatory relationships.
Disciplinary genre | A student can narrate a family event but struggles with a laboratory report. Which support most directly addresses the task difference? | Teach how the report organizes methods, observations and interpretation | Treat all successful narration as proof of report mastery | Diagnose language disorder solely from genre unfamiliarity | Remove scientific content until all casual conversation is perfect | Genres organize meaning differently; explicit instruction can connect existing resources to the new purpose. | One successful genre does not establish all genres, and unfamiliarity alone is not a diagnosis or reason to eliminate content.
Inference | A story states that the ground is wet and people carry umbrellas. A learner concludes that it has probably rained. What skill is illustrated? | Drawing an inference from clues and prior knowledge | Copying an explicitly stated sentence about rainfall | Counting the number of words in the passage | Translating a fixed idiom without context | The conclusion goes beyond the literal statements using related evidence. It remains an interpretation rather than certainty. | No rainfall sentence is stated; word counting and idiom translation do not describe the reasoning shown.
Synthesis | A learner combines findings from two texts into an explanation of language maintenance. Which operation is central? | Integrating relevant information into a coherent account | Repeating a single isolated sentence without connection | Listing two titles as a substitute for relating findings | Treating sources as identical without checking their claims | Synthesis relates information rather than merely reproducing or listing it. | Repetition, disconnected listing and assumed equivalence do not perform the required integration.
Prior knowledge | A child explains irrigation accurately in Punjabi but cannot name the equipment in English. What should be separated? | Conceptual understanding and access to English labels | Intelligence and a supposedly inherently simpler home language | Literacy and nationality as identical attributes | Conversational volume and scientific truth | The child demonstrates relevant knowledge through one language while needing additional forms in another. | Linguistic prestige, nationality and loudness do not measure the distinction illustrated.
Formative assessment | A teacher notices confusion about one connective and changes the next activity to address it. Which assessment purpose is illustrated? | Using current evidence to adjust ongoing instruction | Certifying a final qualification without further teaching | Establishing language dominance from nationality | Diagnosing a disorder from one classroom response | The evidence informs the next teaching step, which is the defining formative use. | The alternatives concern certification, unsupported profiling or premature clinical inference.
Benefits and evidence | A paper reports better task switching in one bilingual sample. Which conclusion is proportionate? | That sample performed better on the measured task under those conditions | Bilingualism improves every cognitive ability in every person | The result establishes that monolingual learners cannot shift tasks | Improved executive performance is required for social language benefits | A task-specific result should be interpreted with its design, uncertainty and comparison group. | Universal superiority, inability in another group and a prerequisite for social value all exceed the finding.
Identity and wellbeing | A student values Punjabi at home but experiences stigma at school. Which conclusion best recognizes context? | Language-related identity and wellbeing depend partly on social recognition | Bilingual status alone guarantees high self-esteem | Home-language value proves that stigma cannot affect participation | School prestige determines which identity is legitimate | Recognition, opportunity and treatment by others shape the experience of language affiliation. | Neither guaranteed wellbeing, immunity from stigma nor institutional authority over identity follows.
Statement appraisal | I. Contextual support can accompany demanding reasoning. II. Conversational fluency guarantees mastery of all academic genres. Which evaluation is correct? | I is accurate; II is inaccurate | Both are accurate | I is inaccurate; II is accurate | Both are inaccurate | A visible complex experiment illustrates I. Genre-specific vocabulary and organization show why conversational ease does not guarantee II. | Treating support as triviality rejects I incorrectly; equating conversation with every academic demand accepts II incorrectly.
''')
cases(4, '''
Culture and individuality | Two Lahore families share Punjabi and a religious affiliation but prefer different appointment arrangements. What should the clinician infer? | Shared affiliations do not eliminate individual variation | One family's preference must be culturally inauthentic | One language identifies a single uniform appointment preference | Religious affiliation determines every communication choice | Cultural affiliations can overlap while histories, resources and preferences differ. | Authenticity judgments and deterministic rules replace individual information with stereotypes.
Idealist emphasis | A researcher focuses on what families believe constitutes respectful communication. Which cultural emphasis is most direct? | Idealist emphasis on meanings and values | Materialist emphasis exclusively on artifacts | A purely phonetic description of vowel formants | An inventory of officially codified spellings | The object of inquiry is beliefs and meanings, the focus of the idealist emphasis in the lecture. | Artifacts, acoustics and spelling conventions address different aspects or analytic levels.
Materialist emphasis | Which study most directly matches a materialist emphasis in the lecture's classification? | Examining everyday work practices, tools and material conditions | Examining only abstract beliefs without practices or conditions | Ranking accents by social acceptability alone | Counting vowels without reference to human activity | Material practices and conditions are central to this emphasis. | The alternatives focus on ideas, prestige judgments or isolated structure rather than the stated cultural emphasis.
Omnibus definition | A description considers a celebration's beliefs, social routines and material objects together. Which approach fits best? | An inclusive or omnibus view of culture | A definition restricted to values alone | A definition restricted to physical objects alone | A claim that culture is identical to nationality | The description deliberately combines ideological, behavioral and material dimensions. | Exclusive definitions omit part of the stated description; nationality is not equivalent to culture.
Culture and nationality | A school uses nationality to assign one assumed home language to every pupil. What information is missing? | The individual pupils' actual linguistic histories and practices | The relative prestige of national flags | A ranking of which nationality has older words | Evidence that all countries possess only one language | National affiliation does not determine one uniform repertoire. Individual inquiry is needed. | Prestige and language-age rankings do not supply the missing profile; the one-language premise is unsound.
Cultural change | A family retains valued traditions while adapting communication to video calls with relatives abroad. Which concept is most evident? | Culture as dynamic practice | Culture as necessarily erased by any adaptation | Nationality as the sole cause of technology use | Culture as identical across all generations | Continuity and change can coexist as people adapt practices to circumstances. | Change does not automatically erase identity, and the case does not establish a single national cause or generational uniformity.
Linguistic relativity | A study asks whether language-specific labels affect attention to one kind of distinction. Which claim is being tested? | A bounded influence of linguistic experience on a defined task | Complete inability to think without a matching single word | A universal ban on learning concepts from other languages | The grammatical superiority of one community | Relativity concerns possible influences; the stated design asks about a specific task. | Determinism, impossibility of learning and superiority claims are stronger and different propositions.
Arbitrariness | Urdu uses kitab and English uses book for similar objects. Why does this illustrate arbitrariness? | The form-meaning relationship is largely conventional rather than naturally necessary | The words have no shared meaning within their communities | Each speaker chooses an unpredictable new label at every use | Written forms cannot be learned through social experience | Different conventional forms can refer to similar meanings without a necessary natural resemblance. | Arbitrariness does not remove shared meaning, stable conventions or learnability.
Meaningful structure | The sentences Ali praised Sara and Sara praised Ali use the same words but express different actor relationships. Which feature matters? | Patterned arrangement contributes to meaning | Every individual sound has a separate dictionary meaning | The words become meaningless because order changes | Linguistic productivity is restricted to memorized word lists | Word arrangement can identify who performed the action and who received it. | This does not assign independent lexical meaning to each sound or imply meaningless combinations and rote-only language.
Duality of patterning | Sounds without individual lexical meanings combine into words, which combine into larger expressions. What property is illustrated? | Duality of patterning | Intergenerational language shift | A single undifferentiated level of meaningful symbols | Institutional codification | The description distinguishes a level of nonmeaningful units from meaningful combinations and their further organization. | Community shift and codification are different processes; the one-level account misses the distinction.
Productivity | A pupil describes a newly invented machine using a sentence never memorized before. Which property is central? | Productivity through patterned combination | Dependence on a fixed inventory of complete memorized sentences | Loss of community conventions | Replacement of grammar by individual random signs | Familiar resources can be combined to express novel situations. | Novelty does not require loss of conventions; the fixed-sentence and random-sign accounts do not explain productive language.
Signed language | Why does Pakistan Sign Language challenge a vocal-only definition of language? | It supports linguistic structure and meaning without requiring the vocal channel | It is merely Urdu spelling performed silently in every utterance | It is a universal gesture code understood without learning | It lacks grammar because its articulators differ from speech | Modality is not the criterion separating language from nonlanguage. Natural signed languages have their own organization. | They are not simply a spoken language's spelling, universal pantomime or grammar-free communication.
Developmental interpretation | A slide presents two-word speech as beginning at 30 months for every child. Which correction is appropriate? | Combinations commonly emerge around age two; individual concerns need contextual evaluation | Thirty months is the exact universal starting date | A younger child using two words must be imitating without meaning | Timing variation makes all developmental assessment impossible | Approximate developmental expectations are useful, but rigid universal dates are misleading. | Earlier combinations can be meaningful, and variation does not remove the value of appropriate developmental assessment.
Vocabulary growth | A handout claims exactly thirteen new words per day until age seventeen. What makes this unsuitable as an individual milestone? | Rates depend on experience, definitions and measurement | Vocabulary stops changing once school begins | Counting words makes every estimate equally precise | All multilingual children have the same exposure profile | A fixed daily figure cannot substitute for an appropriate learning history and developmental profile. | Growth continues; measurement methods matter; multilingual experiences are heterogeneous.
Gender generalizations | A survey in one classroom finds that a particular group uses more standard variants. Which interpretation is justified? | A context-bound association requiring attention to sampling and other influences | A universal biological rule that one gender has better grammar | Proof that all speakers in the group behave identically | Evidence that nonstandard speakers cannot express complex thought | A local pattern is not a universal law or an individual prediction. Consider role, task, networks and sampling. | Biological ranking, complete uniformity and linguistic incapacity do not follow from the observation.
Class and complexity | A teacher calls a working-class dialect simpler and therefore less capable of expressing ideas. What is the central error? | Social status is being confused with linguistic capacity | Lack of institutional prestige proves lack of grammar | Standard spelling is the sole measure of expressive thought | Occupational status determines the length of every sentence | Varieties can differ structurally while all provide systematic resources for expression. | Prestige, spelling and occupation do not establish an inherently deficient language system.
Negative concord | In a variety where negative concord is grammatical, he don't know nothing expresses one negation. How should it be interpreted? | According to the relevant variety's systematic grammar | As necessarily a logical positive because there are two negative forms | As direct proof of a language disorder in that speaker | As a rule permitting all word omissions in every context | Negative concord can use several negative forms to express one semantic negation. | Standard-English editing conventions and formal logic cannot simply be imposed as the grammar of every variety; it is not a clinical diagnosis or unrestricted deletion rule.
Copula distribution | A variety permits copula absence in some contexts. Which finding would a careful analysis require? | The contexts in which overt forms can and cannot be absent | An assumption that every copula can disappear in every tense | A rule that dialect grammar needs no description | A ranking of the speakers' intelligence before analysis | Variable absence has constraints, and describing them distinguishes systematic grammar from an unrestricted omission claim. | The alternatives overgeneralize, deny the need for description or substitute a social judgment for linguistic evidence.
Taboo and context | A topic is avoided at one family gathering but discussed openly in another setting. What does this indicate? | Sensitivity depends on participants and context | The first gathering cannot have real cultural norms | The topic has one universally prohibited meaning | Everyone using the language must share identical restrictions | Taboos are socially situated; norms can differ by relationship, audience and occasion. | Variation does not make a norm unreal, universalize it or establish identical practices among all speakers.
Euphemism | A speaker uses passed away rather than a direct term for death to reduce distress. Which function is intended? | Euphemistic softening of sensitive reference | A change in the speaker's regional pronunciation | A direct test of receptive vocabulary size | A necessary claim that death has not occurred | The choice manages how a sensitive meaning is expressed. Audience and context still determine its effect. | It does not identify accent, measure vocabulary or necessarily deny the event.
Idiom and proverb | Which comparison is accurate? | An idiom has conventional meaning beyond its words; a proverb often conveys general advice or observation | An idiom is any regional accent; a proverb is any long sentence | An idiom must be translated literally; a proverb has no contextual application | Idioms and proverbs are mutually exclusive grammatical diagnoses | The terms concern conventional meaning and communicative function; particular expressions can have overlapping properties. | The alternatives misclassify accents and length, ignore context or turn discourse categories into diagnoses.
Intercultural understanding | A student understands every word in a joke but misses its point because the historical reference is unfamiliar. What is most directly missing? | Relevant shared cultural or background knowledge | Proof of a generalized language disorder | An additional vowel contrast in the student's accent | Evidence that the joke's language has no grammar | Literal word recognition does not guarantee access to allusions and shared expectations. | The case supplies no basis for a clinical diagnosis or an explanation through accent or absent grammar.
Eye contact | A pupil looks away while listening to a teacher and can accurately explain the instruction. Which conclusion is best supported? | Gaze alone was not a sufficient measure of this pupil's understanding | Looking away establishes lack of attention despite the explanation | The pupil represents every member of one national culture | Accurate explanation establishes normal performance on every communication task | The observed comprehension contradicts a simple equation of eye contact with understanding in this encounter. | One behavior cannot diagnose attention, stand for an entire culture or establish every aspect of communication.
Proxemics | A clinician asks whether moving the chair farther away would make an interview more comfortable. Which aspect is directly addressed? | Interpersonal spatial arrangement | Historical origin of a language | Relative frequency of grammatical variants | Translation-equivalent vocabulary totals | Proxemics concerns space and distance in interaction. Preferences depend on people and circumstances. | Language history, grammar counts and vocabulary scoring address different questions.
Cultural humility | A clinician has read about a community but asks the family to correct any mistaken assumptions. What practice is illustrated? | Ongoing reflection and openness to individual experience | Permanent mastery of every practice after reading one summary | Treating the family as a representative sample of all co-nationals | Replacing professional explanation with unquestioned stereotypes | Humility acknowledges limits and creates room for the person's account. | Memorization is not permanent mastery, and an individual should not be made to represent a whole population.
''')
cases(5, '''
Accent and dialect | Two speakers differ only in vowel realization in the sample available; vocabulary and grammar are the same. What is the most specific description of the observed difference? | An accent difference | A demonstrated grammatical dialect difference | A proven boundary between independent languages | A diagnosed expressive-language disorder | The evidence concerns pronunciation, which accent describes most specifically. | Dialect can include accent but is broader; the sample does not establish grammatical differences, separate languages or disorder.
Regional and social patterns | A variant is associated with peer networks in several different districts. Which interpretation is most directly supported? | A social association extending across geographic locations | A boundary fixed by district administration alone | An identical idiolect shared by every individual | A necessary difference between distinct national languages | Social and geographic variation can intersect, and networks can cross administrative boundaries. | District lines alone do not explain the stated distribution; individuals do not have identical idiolects; national-language status is not established.
Idiolect | Two siblings share a regional variety, but one repeatedly uses particular expressions uncommon in the other's speech. Which concept best captures the individual pattern? | Idiolect | National language policy | A separate societal language | Institutional standardization | Idiolect concerns the characteristic resources of an individual. It coexists with shared regional and social patterns. | The observation does not establish policy, an independent language or codification.
Standard status | A school recommends one variety for formal writing. Which conclusion is warranted? | The variety has institutional support for that use | Other varieties lack systematic grammar | The selected variety is biologically superior | Every speaker already uses it in all home interactions | Standard status concerns institutional norms and functions. | It does not demonstrate absence of other grammars, innate superiority or uniform everyday practice.
Codification | A committee publishes agreed spelling conventions and a reference grammar. Which process is most directly illustrated? | Codification | Language attrition | Individual pronunciation convergence | A complete measure of language vitality | Codification formalizes conventions. It may contribute to wider standardization but does not itself measure every social dimension. | Attrition and convergence describe different changes, while vitality needs evidence about use and transmission.
Standardization sequence | Which account best describes a common analytic model of standardization? | Selection, codification, elaboration of functions and acceptance | Hearing, naming, conceptual scoring and clinical discharge | Regional isolation, inevitable superiority, universal uniformity and extinction | Vocabulary loss, sentence loss, identity loss and absence of norms | Standardization is commonly discussed through selecting a norm, describing conventions, extending uses and social acceptance; these are analytic processes, not a universal rigid chronology. | The alternatives confuse clinical procedures, unsupported rankings or language loss with standardization.
Overt prestige | A job interview panel explicitly rewards institutionally preferred pronunciation. Which social value is most directly involved? | Overt prestige | Covert local solidarity only | Receptive vocabulary growth | Mutual intelligibility as the sole criterion | Explicit institutional approval illustrates overt prestige, whether or not the judgment is fair. | Covert prestige concerns local value despite wider stigma; neither vocabulary growth nor a full intelligibility comparison is measured.
Covert prestige | Friends value a local feature that school rules discourage because it signals belonging. Which concept fits? | Covert prestige | Absence of any social value | A published national spelling convention | Proof of defective grammatical learning | A feature can have positive in-group meaning while lacking institutional approval. | Stigma does not remove all value; the case is not codification or evidence of linguistic deficiency.
Speech community | Speakers differ in forms but share expectations about which styles suit particular occasions. What supports treating them as a speech community? | Shared norms of use despite internal variation | Identical performance on every language test | A requirement that only one accent occur | A common passport as sufficient evidence by itself | Shared norms can unite a speech community without uniform linguistic production. | Identical scores, one accent and administrative identity are not necessary or sufficient substitutes for shared practices.
Community of practice | Members of a Lahore debating club develop familiar expressions through repeated joint activity. Which concept is most specific? | Community of practice | A purely hereditary ethnolect | A geographical isogloss | An individually monolingual society | Recurrent shared activity helps shape practices and linguistic identity. | Ancestry and a mapped feature boundary are not the defining evidence, and the last phrase confuses individual and societal levels.
Register and jargon | An SLP explains phonology to colleagues using technical terms, then uses accessible language with parents. Which distinction best describes the change? | Situational register adjustment with different use of jargon | Replacement of one regional dialect because the earlier one lacked grammar | A change in the clinician's first language acquisition history | Language attrition established by avoiding a technical term | Purpose and audience influence register; specialized vocabulary can be appropriate among colleagues but need explanation elsewhere. | No deficit, historical acquisition change or proficiency loss is established.
Style shifting | A student uses local forms with friends and institutional forms in a presentation. What does this demonstrate? | Context-sensitive resources within the student's repertoire | Evidence that the home variety is not a real linguistic system | An obligatory permanent loss of local identity | Two languages necessarily acquired simultaneously | Style shifts show choices related to setting and audience. | They do not establish linguistic invalidity, permanent identity loss or a particular language count and onset.
Convergence and divergence | In one meeting a speaker aligns pronunciation with a partner; in another, the speaker emphasizes local forms to mark distinctiveness. What is the correct order? | Convergence followed by divergence | Divergence followed by convergence | Codification followed by attrition | Vitality followed by historicity | Convergence increases similarity; divergence emphasizes difference. Both can be interactional adjustments. | The reversed pair swaps their meanings; institutional description, loss and historical identity concern different processes.
Continuum | Neighboring communities understand each other easily, but varieties at opposite ends of a region differ considerably. Which concept best fits? | Dialect continuum | A guarantee that all varieties are one politically recognized language | A single isogloss governing every feature | A proof that all speakers use an identical idiolect | Gradual geographic differentiation can yield substantial distance across the whole area. | The continuum alone does not settle political classification, require one feature boundary or imply individual uniformity.
Mutual intelligibility | Speakers understand a neighboring variety better than a distant one, and exposure improves understanding. Which conclusion is appropriate? | Intelligibility is relevant but sensitive to experience and comparison conditions | Intelligibility automatically determines every official language label | All asymmetry must indicate a communication disorder | Exposure is irrelevant to comprehension between varieties | Comprehension depends partly on familiarity and can be asymmetrical; classification also involves social and historical factors. | A single comprehension pattern does not settle status or diagnose a disorder, and the case directly indicates an exposure effect.
Isogloss | A map marks where one pronunciation feature changes, while a vocabulary feature changes elsewhere. What does this illustrate? | Different features can have different isoglosses | Every feature must share an administrative boundary | One feature boundary proves an entirely separate language | Dialect mapping requires ignoring individual variation | An isogloss concerns a specific feature; distributions can overlap without identical borders. | Administrative coincidence, language status and uniformity do not follow from one mapped pattern.
Dialect-informed diagnosis | A child uses a community-appropriate grammatical form that differs from the school's standard. Which procedure is appropriate before a clinical conclusion? | Check variety-specific expectations and the broader communication profile | Count every nonstandard token as a clinical error | Use social class as a direct substitute for assessment | Require permanent suppression of the home variety | Community patterns need to be distinguished from impairment using relevant evidence. | Standard deviation, class stereotypes and identity suppression do not establish a clinical diagnosis.
Language discrimination | An applicant meets the job's communication demands but is rejected because a regional accent is stereotyped as unintelligent. Which issue is illustrated? | Linguistic discrimination | A valid direct measure of reasoning ability | An inevitable consequence of language standardization | Proof that accent and job communication are identical measures | An unsupported social judgment is being used against a capable applicant. | Accent stereotypes are not reasoning tests; standardization does not require unfair exclusion; job demands must be assessed directly.
Regional labels | A client identifies as a Hindko speaker. A slide groups several northern varieties under Punjabi. What should the SLP do? | Record the client's identification and investigate relevant linguistic patterns | Override the identification because one teaching list settles classification | Assume all northern varieties have identical sound and grammar systems | Avoid language history because labels can be contested | Source-sensitive classification and individual history support a useful profile without imposing an umbrella label. | A disputed list is not decisive, related varieties need not be identical, and uncertainty increases the need for inquiry.
Doabi association | Which association is the safest one to use for the term Doabi in this course? | A historical Punjabi association with the Beas-Sutlej Doaba, allowing migration | An exclusive residence rule for every current speaker | A clinical category defined by pronunciation errors | A claim of uniquely oldest status among all regional varieties | The regional association is useful without pretending that migration and variation cease at a map boundary. | Current-address exclusivity, clinical labeling and historical ranking are not warranted by the label.
Oldest-dialect claim | A slide calls one Punjabi variety the oldest but supplies no historical evidence. Which exam explanation is most defensible? | Identify the stated regional term while qualifying the unsupported historical priority | Repeat oldest as a settled fact inferred from the name | Conclude that every other regional variety has no history | Treat historical evidence as irrelevant to an oldest claim | Separate a regional association from a historical claim requiring evidence. | Names do not prove priority, other varieties have histories, and historical evidence is exactly what the superlative requires.
Vitality and codification | A community has an old dictionary but children rarely learn the language. Which distinction matters? | Codification may exist despite threatened transmission | A dictionary guarantees high vitality in every generation | Lack of child acquisition proves there are no de facto norms | Written history prevents future language shift | Documentation and standardization are distinct from active use and transmission. | A dictionary does not guarantee vitality; remaining speakers can retain norms; historical writing does not make shift impossible.
Historicity and autonomy | A community links a variety to a long tradition and regards it as independent of a neighboring standard. Which two criteria are described? | Historicity and autonomy | Reduction and a clinical language disorder | Attrition and an individual silent period | Proxemics and dynamic assessment | Historical identity concerns historicity; perceived independence concerns autonomy. | The alternatives combine different processes and domains that the account does not describe.
De facto norms | A community shares stable expectations for greetings without a published grammar. Which criterion is directly illustrated? | De facto norms | Absence of linguistic conventions | Full official codification | Guaranteed majority-language status | Norms can function through shared practice without official written rules. | Lack of a book does not mean lack of conventions, and shared practice does not establish codification or social majority.
Revitalization | Which action most directly strengthens intergenerational use of a threatened community language? | Community-led opportunities for children to communicate with fluent users | Storing a word list without any new use opportunities | Declaring one variety superior while discouraging its speakers | Assuming loyalty alone guarantees transmission | Documentation can be valuable, but revitalization centers community goals and meaningful continued use. | An archive alone does not create interaction; stigma can undermine use; loyalty without opportunity does not guarantee transmission.
''')
